\import{fcap-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\tag{fcap}
\tag{spin}
\tag{lie}

\taxon{Theorem}
\title{A pinned three-cycle integrates to compact triality}

\p{Let #{G=\Spin(Q_8^+)} be the compact positive-square Clifford
group, and let #{V} denote its complexified vector representation.
There is a smooth outer automorphism #{\tau:G\to G} of exact order
three for which

##{V\circ\tau\simeq S^+,\qquad V\circ\tau^2\simeq S^-.}

Here #{S^+=L(\omega_4)} and #{S^-=L(\omega_3)} use the exterior
convention of \ref{fcap-003B}. These are literal pullbacks of
representations of the compact real group.}

\subtree{
\taxon{Proof}
\p{Choose a real orthonormal basis #{a_i,b_i}, #{1\leq i\leq4},
and set #{u_i=(a_i+\mathrm i b_i)/2}, #{u_i'=\overline{u_i}}.
Since #{B_Q=2\langle\ ,\ \rangle}, these are polar-dual isotropic
bases. In the complexified Clifford algebra,

##{u_i u_j'+u_j'u_i=\delta_{ij},\qquad
u_i u_j+u_j u_i=u_i'u_j'+u_j'u_i'=0.}

Use precisely the quadratic generators of \ref{fcap-002X}:

##{\begin{aligned}
H_i&=u_i u_i'-\tfrac12,\\
E_i&=u_i u_{i+1}',&F_i&=u_{i+1}u_i',&h_i&=H_i-H_{i+1}
&& (i<4),\\
E_4&=u_3u_4,&F_4&=u_4'u_3',&h_4&=H_3+H_4.
\end{aligned}}

They belong to the complexified quadratic Lie algebra
#{\mathfrak g_{\mathbb C}} of \ref{fcap-0037}. The simple roots are
#{\epsilon_i-\epsilon_{i+1}} for #{i<4} and
#{\epsilon_3+\epsilon_4} for node #{4}; node #{2} is central.}

\p{Let #{A} be the corresponding #{D_4} Cartan matrix: its diagonal
entries are #{2}, adjacent-node entries #{-1}, and all others #{0}.
The displayed Clifford relations give

##{\begin{aligned}
[h_i,h_j]&=0,&[E_i,F_j]&=\delta_{ij}h_i,\\
[h_i,E_j]&=A_{ij}E_j,&[h_i,F_j]&=-A_{ij}F_j,
\end{aligned}}

##{\begin{aligned}
(\operatorname{ad}E_i)^{1-A_{ij}}E_j&=0,\\
(\operatorname{ad}F_i)^{1-A_{ij}}F_j&=0\qquad(i\ne j).
\end{aligned}}

The positive-root generation in \ref{fcap-002X}, its lowering
counterpart and the brackets #{[E_i,F_i]} generate all of
#{\mathfrak g_{\mathbb C}}. The Serre presentation and pinned
extension theorem in \citet{Section 21.3, pp. 337--338}{fulton2004representation}
therefore apply to this Lie algebra.}

\p{The permutation #{\sigma=(1\ 3\ 4)}, fixing #{2}, preserves
#{A}. Reindexing each generator family preserves the presentation,
so it defines a unique complex Lie automorphism #{\theta} with

##{\theta(E_i)=E_{\sigma(i)},\qquad
\theta(F_i)=F_{\sigma(i)},\qquad
\theta(h_i)=h_{\sigma(i)}.}

Inverse reindexing supplies its inverse. Its cube fixes all generators,
hence #{\theta^3=1}.}

\p{The compact real form must also be preserved. Let #{\kappa} be
conjugation of the complexified real Clifford algebra. The same
Clifford relations, with the reversed factor order in #{F_4}, give

##{\begin{aligned}
\kappa(H_i)&=u_i'u_i-\tfrac12=-H_i,\\
\kappa(E_i)&=u_i'u_{i+1}=-F_i&& (i<4),\\
\kappa(E_4)&=u_3'u_4'=-F_4.
\end{aligned}}

Also #{\kappa(F_i)=-E_i} and #{\kappa(h_i)=-h_i}. Thus the
antilinear maps #{\kappa\theta} and #{\theta\kappa} agree on
the generators and everywhere. By \ref{fcap-0037}, the
#{\kappa}-fixed quadratic Lie algebra is exactly #{T_1G}.
Consequently #{\theta} restricts to a real Lie automorphism of the
actual compact group's tangent algebra.}

\p{The group #{G} is connected and simply connected by
\ref{fcap-003A}. The general group-to-group integration theorem,
the Second Principle in \citet{pp. 118--119}{fulton2004representation},
therefore integrates this restriction to a smooth homomorphism
#{\tau:G\to G}. Its graph-subgroup proof is the construction in
\ref{fcap-003L} with target #{G}: first projection is a surjective
covering and hence an isomorphism. Integrating #{\theta^{-1}} too
gives the inverse of #{\tau}, because a homomorphism from connected
#{G} is determined by its differential. Indeed, equal differentials
give equality on an exponential neighborhood, which generates #{G}.
The same uniqueness proves #{\tau^3=1}.}

\p{For a highest weight #{\omega_j}, literal pullback along
#{\theta} gives

##{(\omega_j\circ\theta)(h_i)=\delta_{j,\sigma(i)}
=\omega_{\sigma^{-1}(j)}(h_i).}

Since #{\theta} also permutes the positive generators, the new
highest weight is #{\omega_{\sigma^{-1}(j)}}. Starting with
#{V=L(\omega_1)}, the pullbacks therefore have labels
#{\omega_1,\omega_4,\omega_3}: they are #{V,S^+,S^-} in that
order. These are distinct irreducible classes. Inner automorphisms
preserve each representation class, so #{\tau} is outer and
nontrivial; together with #{\tau^3=1}, this proves exact order three.}
}

\p{The outer-class calculation in \ref{fcap-003C} is thereby realized
by a chosen cube-one automorphism. TauCeti's
[Serre diagram automorphism](https://github.com/TauCetiProject/TauCeti/blob/fac34771ae5768f0b0825f660ab909432f04f85a/TauCeti/Algebra/Lie/Presentation/Serre/Automorphism.lean)
formalizes generator reindexing and its order relation on the presented
Lie algebra. Its compact real-form identification and integration to this
Clifford group are additional steps proved above. The tripled-carrier
automorphism discussed in \ref{fcap-003B} remains a different
construction.}
